1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:25,348
They wanted to put me on a horse for the handover. Nigga, I just won
fifty-two cars. Lord Orlan stood beside the white horse outside the
palace gate, holding a ceremonial schedule. The horse ate a leaf off
the hedge. Tradition, Orlan said. Does tradition have a transmission?
Tank bent over laughing. Orlan checked his schedule as though it might
contain an answer about gears.

2
00:00:25,598 --> 00:00:48,909
Shawna stroked the horse's neck. It leaned toward her hand
immediately. Shameless nigga. Hadn't even introduced himself. I like
him, she said. I looked at her. Looked at the horse. Welcome to the
family, nigga. Orlan offered me the reins. I accepted them. The horse
promptly took another leaf. We leaving? I asked. He's eating, Shawna
said.

3
00:00:49,159 --> 00:01:13,205
Nigga, so was I when all this tradition arrived. The groom helped
position him. I mounted. A cheer went up from people who had watched
me drive through considerably worse arrangements, and the horse turned
his head at the noise. Yeah, I told him. Get used to that. Shawna
settled into the open carriage beside me. Tank and the others followed
in the carriage behind.

4
00:01:13,455 --> 00:01:38,988
We started down toward the harbor at a speed that gave every balcony
time to offer an opinion. Workers waved from doorways. Musicians
raised their instruments. Women from Blue Hour shook blue scarves
overhead. Drivers who had spent a month trying to pass me stood
shouting my name while a horse did a perfectly ordinary walk. Sit
kinglier! Tank called. Nigga, I'm sitting. But kinglier.

5
00:01:39,238 --> 00:02:02,057
I straightened. The horse stopped to inspect something in the road.
The whole procession stopped with him. Shawna covered her mouth.
Don't, I told her. I haven't said anything. Your shoulders saying
plenty. The groom clicked his tongue. The horse moved on. Behind us
Tank started cheering again, completely out of proportion to the
accomplishment.

6
00:02:02,307 --> 00:02:26,022
Nigga, I won seven keys and fifty-two cars, and this hairy bastard had
everybody impressed because he resumed walking. The shipyard was open.
All the remaining workers on Luc's list had been found alive, along
with others whose names hadn't reached us. Some had already left the
island. Some stayed to collect wages from the seized race funds.

7
00:02:26,272 --> 00:02:47,121
Some simply sat in the sun with food and a phone, doing nothing a rich
man could turn into an obligation. Lark and Mateo went there together
before the ceremony. They came back with grease on their hands and a
list of people who wanted work at their new garage. Good mechanics? I
asked. Better than good, Lark said.

8
00:02:47,371 --> 00:03:11,590
Nigga, hire them before somebody gets intelligent. Mercedes and
Porsche had taken over the former guesthouse at the eastern quay for
No Second Door. The first thing they removed was the little side
entrance marked STAFF ONLY. They didn't tear out the wall. They
changed where people were greeted and made the main doors wide enough
for everybody's pride. Alina would help run it.

9
00:03:11,840 --> 00:03:35,206
Rocco invested after Porsche made him say the number without smiling
like he expected applause for paying a fair share. Nero and Cassius
claimed the old racing shed. Esteban brought his tools. Inez brought
three cars and an opinion about every shelf. Within an hour, the place
sounded so alive with argument that I could hear it from the road.

10
00:03:35,456 --> 00:03:59,222
Nigga, some buildings need a family fighting over where the wrench
goes before they become home. At the harbor, the Sovereign Sun waited
beneath a forest of flags. Celadon stood on the bridge in his old
captain's coat. Renee stood beside him, one hand on his arm. The crew
lined the decks, clean uniforms, shoulders straight. The white bird
flew above them all.

11
00:03:59,472 --> 00:04:21,752
On the quay, Marcellus had arranged the seven keys in order inside
their case. The real Blue Mercy shone on his hand as he touched the
last one, checking it like a father straightening a son's tie before a
wedding. You good? I asked him. The key is. Nigga, I meant you. He
looked up at the ship. Then at the island behind us.

12
00:04:22,002 --> 00:04:47,064
Yes, he said. I believe I am. Odette joined us before the ceremony.
She spoke quietly enough that the crowd couldn't turn the news into
entertainment. Morcant's body was recovered this morning. His death is
confirmed. Bellario remains in custody on the mainland. He has given
names, payments and the locations of the duke's remaining accounts. I
nodded. The workers?

13
00:04:47,314 --> 00:05:08,785
Everyone on the list is accounted for. That was the part I had needed.
She held out her hand. Captain. I shook it. Commandant. You are
welcome in my harbor. Nigga, even with the car? Her mouth twitched.
Please arrive by one method at a time. I couldn't promise that, so I
kissed her hand instead and let her laugh at the evasion.

14
00:05:09,035 --> 00:05:31,489
A boyish-looking reporter in a very adult suit asked whether he could
interview the horse. He was twenty-seven and, apparently, had spent
the month watching me until ordinary journalism left his body. "Nigga,
ask the horse," I said. "He won't answer." "Then that nigga got better
press discipline than me." Tank leaned toward the microphone.

15
00:05:31,739 --> 00:05:55,378
"Horse says Aiden heavy with greatness." "Nigga," Shawna said, "leave
the horse out of your mouth." The reporter laughed and turned the
camera toward her instead. "Wise nigga," I said. "Always interview the
wife when the men start translating livestock." The handover began at
noon. Orlan read the old race proclamation from a book bound in blue
leather.

16
00:05:55,628 --> 00:06:20,268
The winner of the Grand Prix of Kings would receive the prize fleet
and Isla Gloria, including the palace, the harbor estates and the race
properties, witnessed by the flagship's captain and the assembled
competitors. Aurelio had spent years making the words sound grand
without letting them become true. Nigga, I stood there with all seven
keys. Words were having an excellent day.

17
00:06:20,518 --> 00:06:46,108
Celadon rang the ship's bell. I put the seventh key into the old
harbor lock. It turned smoothly. Marcellus made a small satisfied
sound beside me. Across the water, Celadon turned the second lock on
the flagship's bridge. The ceremonial chain lowered at the harbor
mouth, and the Sovereign Sun moved forward through the opened entrance
while horns sounded from every boat in the bay.

18
00:06:46,358 --> 00:07:09,901
Forty road cars waited along the quay. Twelve racing cars stood
beneath the palace banners. Six yachts answered the flagship's bell.
Isla Gloria opened around us like a hand. Lord Orlan knelt. Nigga, I
had not asked him to do that. The crowd loved it so much he stayed
down long enough to make sure the photographers had found his better
side.

19
00:07:10,151 --> 00:07:34,070
He held up the island deed. To Aiden, he said. Winner of every stage.
Captain of the Grand Prix. Lord of Isla Gloria. Tank made a sound from
somewhere behind me that I can only describe as a man trying to become
a stadium. The crowd answered. Aiden. Aiden. Aiden. I took the deed.
Then I took Shawna's hand and pulled her beside me.

20
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:56,746
Nigga, I told the crowd, I told y'all she wanted the ocean. Shawna
covered her face, laughing. The harbor shook with applause. We walked
the fleet afterward. A blue Ferrari for Shawna. A white Bentley
because she liked the back seat. A silver roadster for Rosa, who
looked at Tank and said, You can drive if you stop fighting the turns.

21
00:07:56,996 --> 00:08:17,062
A gold coupe for Mercedes. A black convertible for Porsche, who
accepted it graciously and immediately asked where the second key was.
Cassius chose a racing car for the restored family team. Inez chose
one faster than his and smiled when he noticed. Nero took no new car.

22
00:08:17,312 --> 00:08:39,318
He stood beside the White Heron with one hand on its roof and said he
already had the one he wanted. Bruno tried to get into three cars
before finding one that respected the width of his shoulders. When he
finally sat down comfortably, he looked up at me with the gratitude of
a man who had spent his whole adult life apologizing to upholstery.

23
00:08:39,568 --> 00:09:02,076
Nigga, this is a real seat. Drive it strong. I'll drive it seated.
Tank wanted the biggest coupe in the fleet. I gave it to him, and he
grinned like I had handed him a new argument against doors. But his
original black Mercedes was still safe in Vegas, shipped home before
the Monaco month with the things we didn't need on the road.

24
00:09:02,326 --> 00:09:25,332
He said he wanted it waiting when we landed. Nigga, that car know me,
he said. This one can learn. Yeah. But the Mercedes knows Rosa's side.
I looked at him. He looked away. Nigga, don't make it poetry. Too
late. The afternoon became a party large enough to need directions.
Food in the square. Music on the quay. Champagne on the yachts.

25
00:09:25,582 --> 00:09:47,023
Cards in the palace garden. Beautiful adults carrying on in rooms that
had previously been reserved for men who confused money with
permission to be dull. Mercedes and Porsche's friends filled the
balconies with color, laughter and the kind of frank flirting that
made rich men suddenly remember how to ask a question politely.

26
00:09:47,273 --> 00:10:10,586
I was offered drinks, cigars, business partnerships, jewelry and three
separate declarations that somebody had never seen a man move through
the world like me. Nigga, I told the third man, you should see me shop
for groceries. He said he had. Clementine's owner had sent him the
video. At sunset, we went to the opera house. The bullet marks were
still outside.

27
00:10:10,836 --> 00:10:36,628
The damaged wall had been covered for the evening, not erased. Inside,
every seat was full. Workers in borrowed jackets sat beside old
aristocrats. Drivers leaned from the balconies. The musicians who had
been dismissed stood in the pit again, looking at one another with a
nervous happiness that made Valentina clear her throat twice before
raising the baton. I sat in the front row.

28
00:10:36,878 --> 00:10:59,724
Tank and Rosa had argued over whether he should sit close enough to
distract her. Rosa won. He sat beside me, which meant the first row
now contained more devoted attention than the stage lights. Shawna
came out in blue. The room stood before she sang. She waited, smiling,
one hand lifted. When the applause softened, she looked directly at
me.

29
00:10:59,974 --> 00:11:22,250
I asked my husband for an ocean, she said. The audience laughed. He
brought the whole neighborhood. The laughter became cheering. She let
it finish. This is for everybody who came home. Then she sang. Nigga,
I have no useful measurement for what her voice did to me that night.
I had watched cars leap water. Held a man over a cliff.

30
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:41,762
Felt blue thunder run through my bones. None of it made the world open
the way that first note did when it filled the room we had fought to
keep. Rosa joined her. Two voices. Different warmth. Different grief.
The same fierce pleasure in being alive enough to make a whole hall
listen.

31
00:11:42,012 --> 00:12:02,015
Tank sat forward with both hands clasped so tightly I could see the
knuckles change color. That woman, he whispered. I know. Nigga, that
woman. I know. On the balcony above us, Celadon held Renee's hand.
Nero sat beside Cassius, Livia's letter folded inside his jacket.

32
00:12:02,265 --> 00:12:25,952
Esteban looked at the stage with tears on his face, and Inez rested a
hand on his shoulder without interrupting anything he needed to feel.
Marcellus turned the Blue Mercy ring once on his finger. Then he
stopped touching it and simply listened. When the final note ended,
the room stayed quiet for one impossibly long breath. Then everybody
stood. I stood first.

33
00:12:26,202 --> 00:12:51,333
Or maybe Tank did. Nigga, we can argue about it when the applause
stops. It didn't stop for a long time. Afterward, Cassius found Inez
in the courtyard behind the theater. I was there with Shawna, stealing
five minutes before the party found us again. We saw him come through
the doors carrying no microphone, no huge arrangement, no borrowed
confidence from the crowd.

34
00:12:51,583 --> 00:13:10,053
Just a ring. He stopped in front of her. I want to finish the
sentence. She looked at his hand. Then at his face. Nigga, finally. He
laughed, nervous and happy, and knelt. She said yes before he finished
asking. Esteban, watching from the doorway, put one hand over his
mouth.

35
00:13:10,303 --> 00:13:33,930
Nero slapped him on the back hard enough to make him cough, then
hugged him when the coughing turned into laughter. Tank came out at
exactly the wrong moment. He saw the ring. Looked at Rosa. Rosa looked
back. Nigga, he whispered to me, the whole island putting pressure on
me. I put an arm around his shoulders. Drive your own race. He nodded
solemnly.

36
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:56,378
Then Rosa kissed his cheek, and the nigga forgot what pressure was.
That night Shawna and I went upstairs to the blue room. The harbor
lights shone through the curtains. Music drifted from the square.
Somewhere below, somebody was still chanting my name with the
determination of a man whose friends had tried and failed to get him
into a taxi.

37
00:13:56,628 --> 00:14:19,084
Shawna took off her earrings and laid them beside Dante's watch. Then
she turned to me. How do you feel, king? I looked at her. At the room
she had chosen before she ever stepped inside it. At the sea beyond
the window. Nigga, I said, like I brought you to the right place. She
came into my arms. You did. We closed the curtains.

38
00:14:19,334 --> 00:14:22,960
The island could keep celebrating without us for a while.
